land, is an island country and microstate in the South Pacific Ocean. It lies within the Micronesia subregion of Oceania, with its nearest neighbour being Banaba about 300 kilometres (190 mi) to the east. Nauru is also known to have one of the highest obesity rates in the world.", "newValue": "Nauru, officially the Republic of Nauru, formerly known as Pleasant Island, is an island country and microstate in the South Pacific Ocean, lying within the Micronesia subregion of Oceania, with its nearest neighbour being Banaba about 300 kilometres to the east. Nauru is also known to have one of the highest obesity rates in the world. The island's phosphate-rich soil was first exploited by the British in 1906, and by 1915, the Phosphate Commission, a consortium of British and Australian companies, had taken control of the phosphate mining. The phosphate mining led to severe environmental degradation and the loss of fertile soil, which had devastating impacts on the local ecosystem and the Nauruan people's way of life. The Republic of Nauru gained independence from Australia on January 31, 1968, and has since struggled to manage its resources and maintain a stable economy. Nauru's isolation and lack of natural resources have made it vulnerable to climate change, and the country has been facing significant challenges in recent years, including rising sea levels and frequent natural disasters.

